Use no tools.
Use only the supplied task directory. Read TASK.txt, GUIDE.txt and the model/algorithm files. Change only the listed editable files. The app and models are trusted read-only adapters. No dependencies, network, file IO, subprocesses or unsafe code in the algorithm. Standard in-memory collections are allowed. Host-injected instruction/skill reads are permitted when required, but other trials, repository code, private graders and reference solutions are outside scope. Do not inspect the compiler binary or its parent directory. Use python3 check.py to submit a visible check; the host compiles and grades a snapshot, at most six submissions. No direct compiler/test invocation is needed. Initial stubs may compile but are deliberately incorrect. Use checks before and after editing. Build artifacts belong to the host. End with a concise result. All inputs and arithmetic fit signed 64-bit values under the stated bounds; results must preserve exact integers. No output or external side effects in run().

Lobo 1.11.2 is a native language. There is no projection/interpreter step. model.lobo declares the immutable DTOs. logic.lobo and its adjacent logic.lobo.ids.json are editable; app/model/util sources are fixed. Each block returns its final expression without a trailing semicolon. Use fn f(x:I64)->I64 {x+1}, let x=...; if condition {a} else {b}, &&/||/!, checked I64 arithmetic, and ==/!= comparisons. Names cannot shadow other visible names or function/type declarations. No assignment, break, continue, return keyword or for statement.
Records: record Pair {left:I64,right:I64}; Pair {left:1,right:2}; pair.left; Pair {...pair,left:3}. Variants: match event {Credit(n)=> expression,Debit(n)=> expression,Lock=> expression,Unlock=> expression}. Every case must occur. Expected types infer case owners. Parenthesize complex match subjects: match (cursor.best) {...}.
Lists: List<T>, List::empty<I64>(), List::prepend(value,list), List::length(list), List::head(list), List::tail(list). Head/tail require nonempty. Prepend reverses order; the supplied util.reverse<T> restores order and may infer T. Lists are persistent; no indexing, append or mutation operation.
Counted loops carry one typed value: fold(i in n,total:I64=0) {total+i}. The index runs 0..n-1; zero iterations return the initial state. For multiple variables, carry a record: fold(i in List::length(items),state:Cursor=Cursor {pending:items,total:0}) {Cursor {pending:List::tail(state.pending),total:state.total+List::head(state.pending)}}. State-driven loop: while(state:State=initial; condition_using_state) {new_state}. To stop early, carry a Bool and test it in the loop condition. Finite execution fuel/heap apply; do not use deep recursive traversal.
Bytes: Bytes::length(data), Bytes::at(data,index) returns 0..255, Bytes::slice(data,start,count), Bytes::empty(), Bytes::push(data,value). Guard bounds before at/slice. List<Bytes> is supported. Remainder uses %, division truncates toward zero. Ordinary expected failures are values in the declared DTO, not runtime faults.
New functions/records/variants need identity entries. Existing IDs must stay stable. After adding declarations, run the explicit authoring command below (it only updates the managed identity map). Then use python3 check.py.

/home/luis/dev/lobo-ar/lobo-lang/benchmarks/private-evidence/matched-study-20261123/freeze/lobo identities sync logic.lobo --source model.lobo --source util.lobo

INPUT DOCUMENTATION v1 (identical across languages). Inputs are JSON objects. Integers below are JSON integers, not strings, floats or booleans. Arrays preserve order. The harness converts this wire representation to the run signature; this is not language syntax.
Fields: limit: integer in [0,64]; data: even-length hexadecimal string encoding at most512 raw bytes, two hexadecimal digits per byte. Input hex accepts either letter case; output bytes use lowercase hex. Decode data before calling run: the string is not UTF-8 payload text, a decimal number, or one byte per digit. An empty string encodes zero bytes. All offsets and lengths in the program count decoded bytes.

Implement run(data,limit)->Summary(frames,consumed,error). data is bytes of length<=512;0<=limit<=64. Each frame is one unsigned length byte n, followed by n payload bytes, followed by one checksum byte equal to (13+sum(payload))%256. Empty payload is valid and needs checksum13. Parse from left to right, retaining payloads in input order. At a frame start: first reject n>limit with error3 EVEN IF payload/checksum is missing; otherwise missing payload/checksum is error1; otherwise wrong checksum is error2. Stop on the first error. consumed is the offending frame's START offset; retain only earlier valid frames. With no error, consumed=len(data),error=0. Empty input succeeds. frames is a list of bytes values, not decoded text.
Task family: frames. Source-only comprehension. Return one JSON object: predictions (SIX complete normalized Summary objects for the inputs below); witness (any valid input yielding {"frames": ["07", "08090a"], "consumed": 8, "error": 0}; unspecified output fields can vary); direct_calls (application functions directly called by run, ignoring constructors/builtins/standard-library methods and util.reverse); impacted (functions in logic whose output can change for identical direct arguments if length_ok additionally rejects length==3; include the edited predicate itself); fault_location (the predicate responsible for that policy); counterexample (any valid input distinguishing original from that mutant). Use plain function names. Output byte values as lowercase hex. Optional top-level notes is a string. No tools. Return JSON only.
Inputs: [{"data": "000d", "limit": 4}, {"data": "0301020313", "limit": 4}, {"data": "01041102050618", "limit": 3}, {"data": "01041102050619", "limit": 3}, {"data": "05", "limit": 4}, {"data": "0104110301", "limit": 4}]
